<spell-notebook "slider usage">
	<header level="1">
		Slider
		<header>A slider allows users to select values within a range.</>
	</>
	<slider-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#events">Events</>
				<ui-item link="#forms">Forms</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
				<ui-item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (values, labels, events, forms), keyboard, accessibility, framework snippets. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write `<ui-slider>` and name it with `aria-label` or a `<label for>`. Fomantic's settings are attributes:

			- `min` / `max` / `step` default to Fomantic's 0, 20 and 1; `step="0"` allows any value.
			- `value` is Fomantic's `start`; a `range` adds `end`. The attributes are the starting (and reset) values; the
			  `value` / `end` properties are the live ones.
			- `labeled` shows a label per step (thinned out on a short track), `ticked` adds ticks, `align` moves them
			  (`bottom`; `right` on a `direction="vertical"` slider).
			- `step-labels` replaces the numbers: one label per step, from `min` (Fomantic's `labelType: 'letter'`,
			  `letters` and `interpretLabel`, written out). It's also what the thumb speaks.
			- `smooth` lets the thumb glide under the pointer while the value still snaps.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			`ui-input` fires on every move (Fomantic's `onMove`) and `ui-changed` when a key press or a drag ends (its
			`onChange`), both with `{ value, end }`. Setting `value` inside a `ui-input` handler wins, so you can clamp or
			reject a move. `step-labels` is also a property:
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const size = document.querySelector("#size") // <ui-slider id="size" labeled max="4" aria-label="Size">
			size.stepLabels = ["XS", "S", "M", "L", "XL"]
			size.addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => console.log(event.detail.value))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Forms">
		<markdown>
			A slider is a form-associated element.

			- With a `name`, it submits `value`; a `range` submits two entries under that name (start, then end):
			  `formData.getAll(name)`.
			- A form reset puts back the starting `value` / `end` (the attributes).
			- `disabled` leaves it out of the form; `readonly` still submits.
		</>

		<section label="In a form" sticky collapsible dividing>
			<example description="A price range, submitted as two `price` entries.  Move it, then reset the form.">
				<html>
					<ui-form>
						<form>
							<label for="slider-price">Price</label>
							<ui-slider id="slider-price" name="price" range labeled max="100" step="10" value="20" end="60"></ui-slider>
							<ui-button type="reset" icon="undo" appearance="basic">Reset</ui-button>
						</form>
					</ui-form>
				</html>
			</example>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			| Key                 | Action                                               |
			| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
			| Arrow keys          | Move the thumb one step the way the arrow points     |
			| Page Up / Page Down | Move two steps                                       |
			| Home / End          | Move to the thumb's lowest / highest position        |
			| Tab                 | Move to the next thumb (a `range` has two), then out |

			On a `direction="vertical"` slider the minimum is at the top, so Arrow Down increases; on a `reversed` one Arrow Left does.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			Each thumb is an ARIA `slider` (the APG pattern) with `aria-valuemin`, `aria-valuemax` and `aria-valuenow`, and
			`aria-orientation` when `direction="vertical"`.

			- A single slider's thumb is named by the host's `aria-label` or a `<label for>`. A `range` is a group named
			  that way, with two thumbs, "Minimum" and "Maximum" (translatable); each stops at the other.
			- Step labels are hidden from assistive tech: the thumb's value is what's announced.
			- `readonly` stays focusable and announced but can't change; `disabled` leaves the tab order.
			- If the element can't render, its native fallback is the browser's range input (two for a `range`), so the
			  value can still be set and submitted; Fomantic's track and thumbs, labels, `vertical` / `reversed` and
			  `step-labels` are dropped.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>`value` / `end` are plain attributes and `stepLabels` a property; listen for `ui-input` or `ui-changed`.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-slider name="volume" max={10} value={volume} onui-input={(event) => setVolume(event.detail.value)} />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-slider name="volume" max="10" :value="volume" :stepLabels="labels" @ui-input="(event) => (volume = event.detail.value)" />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-slider
			  name="volume"
			  max="10"
			  value={volume()}
			  stepLabels={labels}
			  on-ui-moved={(event) => setVolume(event.detail.value)}
			/>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
